---
import Install from '../components/Install.astro';
import Iso from '../components/Iso.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { APP_ID, IMAGES, ORG_ID } from '../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'Already on Fedora Silverblue or Kinoite? One bootc switch moves you to Pulsar. Fresh machine? Boot the weekly ISO. NVIDIA works with Secure Boot left on.';

const ENROL = 'sudo mokutil --import /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der\nsudo systemctl reboot';
const TAKE = `mokutil --test-key /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der   # ...is already enrolled\nsudo bootc switch ${IMAGES.nvidia}:latest\nsudo systemctl reboot`;
const CHECK = 'modinfo -F signer nvidia\nnvidia-smi';
const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Install Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/install', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const MOK = ['Enroll MOK', 'View key 0', 'Continue', 'Yes', 'password', 'reboot'];
---

<Base title="Install Pulsar — one bootc switch from Silverblue, or an ISO" crumb="Install" description={DESCRIPTION} path="/install" jsonLd={JSONLD}>
  <main id="main" class="page">
    <arc-container size="lg">
      <arc-page-header border>
        <h1 slot="heading">Install</h1>
        <p slot="description">One command if you are already on Silverblue; one download if you are not. Either way the machine never compiles anything.</p>
      </arc-page-header>

      <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="two-ways">
        {/* the outline needs a level between the page's h1 and the two
            routes' h3s; the design does not want a visible one */}
        <h2 class="sr-only" id="two-ways">Two ways to install</h2>
        <Install />
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="secure-boot">
        <h2>Enrol the key, once</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <p class="lede">
          The nvidia image's driver is signed with Pulsar's key so Secure&nbsp;Boot can stay on.
          Your firmware has to be told to trust that key once, through a blue screen called
          MokManager on the next&nbsp;boot.
        </p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li>
            <div class="step-body">
            <h3>Import the certificate</h3>
            <p>
              <code>mokutil</code> asks for a password you will retype at the firmware screen on the
              next boot — used once, then never again.
            </p>
            <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="enrol" code={ENROL}></arc-code-block>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-body">
            <h3>Answer MokManager</h3>
            <p>The next boot stops in a blue firmware screen. The sequence is:</p>
            <p class="mok">
              {MOK.map((step, index) => (
                <>
                  <arc-kbd>{step}</arc-kbd>
                  {index < MOK.length - 1 && <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
                </>
              ))}
            </p>
            <p class="hint">
              Miss the prompt and nothing breaks — the enrolment just doesn't happen. Run the import
              again.
            </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-body">
            <h3>Switch to the nvidia image</h3>
            <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="switch" code={TAKE}></arc-code-block>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-body">
            <h3>Check the driver loaded</h3>
            <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="check" code={CHECK}></arc-code-block>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="Lost the key later?">
          A BIOS update can clear the whole MOK list. On the nvidia image the recovery is GNOME
          Software's own Secure Boot prompt: it enrols <code>/etc/pki/akmods/certs/public_key.der</code>,
          and a boot-time unit keeps that file equal to <code>MOK.der</code>, so the prompt enrols the
          right key. If you would rather not trust a stranger's key at all, the standard image needs
          none — and the repo is MIT-licensed to fork with your own.
        </arc-alert>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="first-login">
        <h2>When you log in</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <p>
            A fresh account comes up in Pulsar’s own theme. <code>pulsar theme list</code> shows the
            rest, <code>pulsar theme set gruvbox</code> switches the whole desktop, and{' '}
            <code>pulsar theme revert</code> gives you stock GNOME. If you rebased an account you had
            already made your own, Pulsar leaves it exactly as it was and says once that themes are
            there.
          </p>
          <p>
            The apps (Steam, Heroic, a video and a music player, the stock GNOME set and a few more)
            install on the first boot that has a network. A machine you rebased onto Pulsar gets
            anything new with <code>sudo pulsar setup apps</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="updates">
        <h2>Updates</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <p>
              You are told, and nothing installs itself. A few minutes after you log in, and every
              six hours after that, Pulsar checks whether a newer build is published and sends one
              desktop notification per build.
            </p>
            <p>
              <code>sudo pulsar update</code> fetches it, and it takes effect on your next restart.
              GNOME Software's automatic updates and bootc's update timer are both off on purpose:
              the first cannot read an image with layered packages, and the second reboots on
              its&nbsp;own.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              Layered anything with <code>rpm-ostree install</code>? Use <code>pulsar update</code>{' '}
              rather than <code>bootc upgrade</code> — <a href="/cli#update">why, on the CLI page</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="bare-metal">
        <h2>Starting from bare metal</h2>
        <arc-divider class="h-rule" align="left" variant="line-gradient"></arc-divider>
        <Iso />
      </section>
    </arc-container>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 var(--space-xl);
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    counter-reset: step;
  }
  .steps > li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 3rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-md);
    max-width: 52rem;
  }
  .steps > li::before {
    counter-increment: step;
    content: counter(step);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    text-shadow: var(--glow-xs);
    line-height: var(--heading-lh);
  }
  .step-body {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .steps h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .steps arc-code-block {
    display: block;
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .mok {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .arrow {
    color: var(--text-ghost);
  }
  arc-alert {
    display: block;
    max-width: 52rem;
  }
  .two {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    max-width: 72rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .two {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-md);
    }
    .steps > li {
      grid-template-columns: 2rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
